# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (F2160).
#
# audit/FINDINGS.md is a DATA LEDGER — one finding per line, pipe-delimited,
# parsed by scripts/findings-ledger-gate.sh. Prettier treated it as prose and
# silently rewrote its contents: a row naming two snake_case identifiers reads
# as markdown emphasis, so `created_utc … entity_redirects` came back out as
# `created*utc … entity*redirects`. Ten identifiers were corrupted this way
# before anyone noticed, including `node_modules`.
#
# That is a formatter editing the evidence. It also made prettier
# NON-IDEMPOTENT on this file — two `--write` passes were needed before
# `--check` would agree, which is the tell that the rewrite was lossy rather
# than cosmetic. And a reflow that wrapped a long row would break the gate's
# line-oriented parse outright.
#
# A ledger has no formatting to enforce. Leave it alone.
audit/FINDINGS.md
audit/COVERAGE.md
audit/DESIGNS.md

# # CRAVE.md is the canonical MAP — dense with identifiers (APP_GUARD became
# APP*GUARD under an emphasis-span rewrite) and env-var globs (REEXTRACT_*
# grew backslashes). Same class as the ledgers: a record, not prose.
CRAVE.md

plans/** is ARCHAEOLOGY (F2504). Same corruption class as the ledger above:
# 04-CONSENSUS-ROADMAP.md:91 was written as a `page-switch-*.md` GLOB and
# prettier rewrote the `*` into `_` as markdown emphasis — `page-switch-_.md`,
# a path that matches nothing. A formatter must not edit a record of the past,
# and these files carry globs, identifiers and command lines throughout.
plans/

# A FINGERPRINTED PROMPT IS A RECORD, NOT PROSE (D5, 2026-08-15).
#
# Every rule in `prompt-rule-release.ts` derives its VERSION from a sha256 of
# its own .md text, and an unlisted fingerprint fails the app's boot loudly by
# design. Prettier reformatting one of these files therefore does not tidy it —
# it BRICKS the process until someone appends a release entry for a "rule
# change" that changed no word the judge reads. That already happened once:
# word-vocabulary-lanes.ts still carries the note that both v2 entries had to be
# re-pointed at the prettier-formatted text before their first commit, "the only
# text that can be committed here", which is a formatter dictating the contents
# of a ledger.
#
# The same reasoning as the ledgers above, one level down: the identity of these
# files IS their bytes. Leave them alone, and the fingerprint means what it says
# — the rule text as authored.
apps/api/src/modules/external-integrations/llm/prompts/
